In this lesson, we'll look at how to pick the right data display for the kind of information you've got. The trick is to start by asking what type of data you have, and what you actually want to show. If your data is categorical, meaning it's just groups or labels, reach for a bar chart when you want to compare counts or percentages across categories. A pie chart works when those categories add up to a whole, like one hundred percent. And if you've got two categorical variables, line them up in a two-way table. Now if you're dealing with one numerical variable, you want a histogram for the shape of a continuous distribution, a dot plot when your data set is small and you want every value visible, a stem-and-leaf plot when you want to keep the actual numbers while still seeing the shape, and a boxplot for a quick five-number summary or to compare groups. If you've got two numerical variables, a scatterplot is your friend for showing the relationship between them. And finally, if your data is collected over time, put time on the x-axis and use a line graph. Now let's see it in action.